#!/usr/bin/env python
"""
openci-tf config0-addon stage runner.

One script, one STAGE env var per execgroup order of the openci_tf_install
stack. The stack (run.py) declares the stages as scheduled jobs; each job's
execgroup order runs this script inside the tenant config0-worker Lambda with
the stage selected by STAGE and the lifecycle side selected by METHOD
(create|destroy). The Lambda ships python, boto3, and requests and nothing
else (no git, no tofu), so this script owns only the stages that need exactly
that. The tofu stages (ecr, deploy, and their destroys) run openci-tf's own
installer in the engine's CodeBuild through the openci-tf-addon-tofu execgroup
(_chrootfiles/var/tmp/openci-tf/openci-tf-addon-tofu.py).

Every thing a stage creates is recorded as ONE resource row of a specific
resource_type, stamped with the add-on identity ``addon_id`` (the parent
``addon`` record's _id, md5("addon:openci_tf")) plus the install run's
project_id / schedule_id / run_id (user rule 2026-09-06: cleanup by identity
query). Removal never reads a saved field list to decide WHAT to delete: each
destroy stage QUERIES the rows of its resource_type by addon_id and deletes the
row once its thing is torn down - by this stage's own API call, or by the
CodeBuild destroy order that ran (and had to succeed) right before it. A row
whose thing is already gone converges (the row is deleted, nothing else).

Every create stage writes its cleanup-identity ROW BEFORE it does the external
provider action (finding M3): a QHost write failure after a provider success
must still leave a discoverable row, or removal (which QUERIES the rows) cannot
find the real thing and a retry can leak a duplicate. The provider actions are
idempotent on their natural key (SSM put_parameter Overwrite, keyed DynamoDB
put), so a retry finds the row already present (upsert) and re-does the same
thing. The one id not known up front is the GitHub webhook's hook id, so its row
keys on the callback URL we control and the hook id is filled in after the POST.

Stages (create):
    token     the ssm_parameter row (purpose=clone_token) is written FIRST, then
              the GitHub clone token (encrypted run environment, never order
              params) is written to SSM /openci-tf/clone-token/<name> as a
              SecureString
    register  the github_webhook row (keyed on the callback URL), the two
              settings_item rows (repo + account), and the two install-namespace
              ssm_parameter rows (webhook_secret, webhook_hook_id) are written
              FIRST; THEN install/register_repo.py from the pinned openci-tf
              source (empty-repo initialization, webhook secret, repo plus
              hub-alias settings, webhook create/reconcile; hook_id recorded in
              SSM) creates those things idempotently, and _record_registration
              confirms them and fills the webhook row's decrypted hook id
    record    the parent addon record (deploy outputs + registration values +
              attempt_id + addon_id) through the resource JSON channel

Stages (destroy), placed by openci_tf_destroy in the contract order
(registration, deploy, image, ECR repository, SSM parameters LAST):
    register            github_webhook rows: close recorded write-back /
                        mutation PRs, delete each webhook (by its recorded hook
                        id, or - for a row whose install failed before the hook
                        id was recorded - by the callback URL the row carries);
                        settings_item rows: delete each item
    openci_tf_deploy    the tofu deploy / image-copy / ecr CodeBuild destroy
    ecr_image           order already ran (and had to succeed) before this
    ecr_repository      Lambda order: delete the rows
    token               ssm_parameter rows: delete each parameter (the clone
                        token among them, the last external side effect)

The pinned openci-tf source (OPENCI_TF_REPO_URL at OPENCI_TF_GIT_REF, a full
commit sha) is fetched as GitHub's archive tarball over HTTPS and verified
against the sha from the archive itself - no git binary is involved.

The GitHub clone token is read ONLY from the GITOPS_TOKEN encrypted run
environment or tenant SSM. It is never printed, logged, or written to an order
or resource surface.

